{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-130104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4xy+5{y}^{2}$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-1,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-4xy+5{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-4xy+5{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2x-4y)\\,i+(10y-4x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1) & =(2(-1)-4(-1))\\,i+(10(-1)-4(-1))\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨2,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),\\,y-(-1)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dc253d4cb3dacb22878b3350299739e9114e870c5d97d802325bca9ce6f56ca4"} {"question" "q-130104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨3,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}+xy+2x$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}+xy+2x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}+xy+2x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2{y}^{2}+y+2)\\,i+(4xy+x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1) & =(2(1)^{2}+(1)+2)\\,i+(4(1)(1)+(1))\\,j \\\\  & =5\\,i+5\\,j \\\\  & =⟨5,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨5,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dc253d4cb3dacb22878b3350299739e9114e870c5d97d802325bca9ce6f56ca4"} {"question" "q-72377" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72377" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-5,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨4,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨5,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72377" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨5,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1),$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-1,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,0) & =\\frac{2(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2(0)}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\  & =(-1)i+0\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,0)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),\\,y-0⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dc253d4cb3dacb22878b3350299739e9114e870c5d97d802325bca9ce6f56ca4"} {"question" "q-130096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨0,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-x+y,$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-x+y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }i+(2xy+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =((-1)^{2}-1)\\,i+(2(1)(-1)+1)\\,j \\\\  & =0\\,i-j \\\\  & =⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-(-1)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dc253d4cb3dacb22878b3350299739e9114e870c5d97d802325bca9ce6f56ca4"} {"question" "q-130094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x{y}^{2}+y)\\,i+(2y{x}^{2}+x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,2) & =(2(1)(2)^{2}+(2))\\,i+(2(2)(1)^{2}+(1))\\,j \\\\  & =10\\,i+5\\,j \\\\  & =⟨10,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨10,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dc253d4cb3dacb22878b3350299739e9114e870c5d97d802325bca9ce6f56ca4"} {"question" "q-72371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-10,15⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨10,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨15,10⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨5,10⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨8,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨10,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)={f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)i-{f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)j \\\\  & =(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)j \\\\ t(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))j \\\\  & =i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dc253d4cb3dacb22878b3350299739e9114e870c5d97d802325bca9ce6f56ca4"} {"question" "q-130198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\,i+j"] ["math" "-i-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+j"] ["math" "i+2\\,j"] ["math" "i-j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "i-j"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve $2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy=1-3x.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy & =1-3x \\\\ 2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,1)={f}_{y}(1,1)i-{f}_{x}(1,1)j\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x)j \\\\  & =(4y{x}^{3}-4x)i-(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4y+3)j \\\\ t(1,1) & =(4(1)(1)^{3}-4(1))i-(6(1)^{2}(1)^{2}-4(1)+3)j \\\\  & =-5\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-5\\,j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dc253d4cb3dacb22878b3350299739e9114e870c5d97d802325bca9ce6f56ca4"} {"question" "q-72463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-5\\,j"] ["math" "5\\,i-5\\,j"] ["math" "5\\,i-j"] ["math" "i"] ["math" "i+5\\,j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5\\,j"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2xy+9.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2xy+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,2)={f}_{y}(-1,2)\\,i-{f}_{x}(-1,2)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-2x)\\,i-(2x-2y)\\,j \\\\ t(-1,2) & =(2(2)-2(-1))\\,i-(2(-1)-2(2))\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $6\\,i+6\\,j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dc253d4cb3dacb22878b3350299739e9114e870c5d97d802325bca9ce6f56ca4"} {"question" "q-72400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\,i-6\\,j"] ["math" "2\\,i-j"] ["math" "6\\,i+6\\,j"] ["math" "i+6\\,j"] ["math" "i-j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6\\,i+6\\,j"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-37516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve $2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=8xy+2-8e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,e).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-37516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =8xy+2-8e \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy & =2-8e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2-8e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,e)={f}_{y}(1,e)\\,i-{f}_{x}(1,e)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x}{y}-8x)i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8y)\\,j \\\\ t(1,e) & =(\\frac{2(1)}{e}-8(1))i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-8(e))\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-37516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dc253d4cb3dacb22878b3350299739e9114e870c5d97d802325bca9ce6f56ca4"} {"question" "q-37516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-37516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(1-4e)\\,i+(\\frac{2}{e}-8)j"] ["math" "(2-8e)\\,i+(2-8e)j"] ["math" "(2e-8)\\,i+(\\frac{e}{2}-8)j"] ["math" "(8-2e)\\,i+(\\frac{8}{e}-2)j"] ["math" "(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-37516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve $x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}=e-y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1} & =e-y \\\\ x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,0)={f}_{y}(1,0)i-{f}_{x}(1,0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)j \\\\  & =(2xy{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+1)i-{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\ t(1,0) & =(2(1)(0){e}^{0+1}+1)i-{e}^{0+1}\\,j \\\\  & =i-e\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-e\\,j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dc253d4cb3dacb22878b3350299739e9114e870c5d97d802325bca9ce6f56ca4"} {"question" "q-130200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-j"] ["math" "2\\,i"] ["math" "2e\\,i+j"] ["math" "i+{e}^{2}\\,j"] ["math" "i-e\\,j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "i-e\\,j"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At which points on the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2y-2x$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $-i+j?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-2)\\,i-(2x+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $-i+j,$ we must have $t\\cdot (-i+j)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot (-i+j) & =0 \\\\ ((2y-2)\\,i-(2x+2)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) & =0 \\\\ -(2y-2)-(2x+2) & =0 \\\\ -2y-2x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-x)^{2} & =2(-x)-2x \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =-4x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=2,$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,2).$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=0,$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dc253d4cb3dacb22878b3350299739e9114e870c5d97d802325bca9ce6f56ca4"} {"question" "q-130217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-2,0)$ and $(0,2)$"] ["text" "$(-2,0)$ only"] ["text" "$(-2,2)$ and $(0,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,0)$ only"] ["text" "$(0,2)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-2,2)$ and $(0,0)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At which points on the curve ${x}^{2}+2xy=-5y+4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy & =-5y+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2xy+5y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy+5y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy+5y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy+5y)j \\\\  & =(2x+5)\\,i-(2x+2y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((2x+5)\\,i-(2x+2y)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -(2x+2y) & =0 \\\\ x & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-y$ into the equation of our curve gives:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy+5y & =4 \\\\ (-y)^{2}+2(-y)y+5y & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+5y & =4 \\\\ -{y}^{2}+5y-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-4) & =0 \\\\ y & =1\\,\\text{or}\\,y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $y=1$, we get $x=-1$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,1).$\n\n- If $y=4$, we get $x=-4$ and the corresponding point is $(-4,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dc253d4cb3dacb22878b3350299739e9114e870c5d97d802325bca9ce6f56ca4"} {"question" "q-72440" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72440" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,1)$ and  $(-4,4)$ only"] ["text" "$(-1,4)$ and  $(-4,1)$ only"] ["text" "$(-1,4)$ only"] ["text" "$(1,1)$ and  $(-2,4)$ only"] ["text" "$(1,1)$ and  $(4,-4)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72440" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-1,1)$ and  $(-4,4)$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}-4xy=-5{x}^{2}+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy & =-5{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})j \\\\  & =2(y-2x)\\,i-(10x-4y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ (2(y-2x)\\,i-(10x-4y)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2(y-2x) & =0 \\\\ y & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=2x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-4x(2x)+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=1$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dc253d4cb3dacb22878b3350299739e9114e870c5d97d802325bca9ce6f56ca4"} {"question" "q-72412" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72412" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2)$"] ["text" "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$"] ["text" "$(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$"] ["text" "$(1,3)$ and $(3,-2)$"] ["text" "$(3,2)$ and $(-1,3)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72412" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}+2xy={x}^{2}+8$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2xy & ={x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=8.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2})j \\\\  & =2(y+x)\\,i-2(y-x)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ (2(y+x)\\,i-2(y-x)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -2(y-x) & =0 \\\\ y & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x)^{2}+2x(x)-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=2$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=-2$, we get $y=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dc253d4cb3dacb22878b3350299739e9114e870c5d97d802325bca9ce6f56ca4"} {"question" "q-72410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(0,2)$ and $(1,2)$"] ["text" "$(0,2)$ only"] ["text" "$(1,2)$ and $(2,1)$"] ["text" "$(2,0)$  and $(0,2)$"] ["text" "$(2,2)$ and $(-2,-2)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-72410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(2,2)$ and $(-2,-2)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At which point on the curve ${x}^{2}y=-8x-4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =-8x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}y+8x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+8x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+8x)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+8x)\\,j \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,i-(2xy+8)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j,$ we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}\\,i-(2xy+8)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -(2xy+8) & =0 \\\\ xy+4 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{4}{x}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =-8x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}(-\\frac{4}{x}) & =-8x-4 \\\\ -4x & =-8x-4 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $x=-1,$ we get $y=4,$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dc253d4cb3dacb22878b3350299739e9114e870c5d97d802325bca9ce6f56ca4"} {"question" "q-130223" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130223" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,4)"] ["math" "(-2,3)"] ["math" "(-3,3)"] ["math" "(1,-12)"] ["math" "(2,-5)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "q-130223" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-1,4)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/state.json" "file_sha256" "b28b18903d00bbac18ef92306064ba42bba2bdc793cd77a410641f84eef771db"} {"question" "e-3362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x,y) = 6x^2+2y^2 - 2xy^2,$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (3,3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/example-3362.json" "file_sha256" "8df923456eeec93843769698c42d24f623db01a7925450b217bac5d7dc36fd65"} {"question" "e-3362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y) = k$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0 \\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (3,3)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(12x-2{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(4y-4xy)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇\\,f(3,3) & =(12(3)-2(3)^{2})i+(4(3)-4(3)(3))j \\\\  & =18\\,i-24\\,j \\\\  & =⟨18,-24⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the level curve that passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)=(3,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(3,3)\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨18,-24⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can leave our answer in this form. Or, if we want to leave our final answer in Cartesian form, we expand the dot product and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18(x-3)-24(y-3) & =0 \\\\ 18x-24y+18 & =0 \\\\ 3x-4y+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/assets/102f55fe0add794d3ee0f9f044b5e35c4403d4f160fe36e97d737d33ef3abb68.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/example-3362.json" "file_sha256" "8df923456eeec93843769698c42d24f623db01a7925450b217bac5d7dc36fd65"} {"question" "e-3363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve $x^2-3xy+4y=2.$ Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/example-3363.json" "file_sha256" "9094a3b590a6c940c936c157d37ae2c38b5d9c4a7907218609e074a98816b5fe"} {"question" "e-3363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, if we set $f(x,y) = x^2-3xy+4y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1) = f_y(1,1) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(1,1) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(-3x+4)\\text{ }i-(2x-3y)\\text{ }j \\\\ t(1,1) & =(-3(1)+4)\\text{ }i-(2(1)-3(1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =1\\,i-(-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf i +\\mathbf j,$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/assets/0114bf524d085402bd27178d687a5faf176b7147c8e8726b84042b17823b6b0b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/example-3363.json" "file_sha256" "9094a3b590a6c940c936c157d37ae2c38b5d9c4a7907218609e074a98816b5fe"} {"question" "e-3364" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At which points on the curve $6xy+3y^2 =7+ y - 3x^2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{j}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/example-3364.json" "file_sha256" "0857bf6e92a6226db4f57b3b7b6d1286e29c77e7fb052aff0d9e9d79004adf96"} {"question" "e-3364" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = 6xy+3y^2 - y + 3x^2 ,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=7.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x,y) = f_y(x,y) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x,y) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6x+6y-1)\\,i-(6y+6x)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j},$ we must have $\\mathbf t \\cdot \\mathbf j = 0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((6x+6y-1)\\,i-(6y+6x)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -6y-6x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6x(-x)+3(-x)^{2} & =7+(-x)-3{x}^{2} \\\\ -6{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & =7-x-3{x}^{2} \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if $x=7,$ we get $y=-7$ and the corresponding point is $(7,-7).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/example-3364.json" "file_sha256" "0857bf6e92a6226db4f57b3b7b6d1286e29c77e7fb052aff0d9e9d79004adf96"}] "usage" {"e-3363" [] "e-3364" [] "e-3362" []} "basis" 2468 "retractions" [[17592186124173 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y) = 6x^2+2y^2 - 2xy^2$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (3,3)$."] [17592186124173 :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = k\n$$\n\nat the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0 \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (3,3)\n$$\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathrm{\\nabla } \\, f ( x , y ) & = {f}_{x} ( x , y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} + {f}_{y} ( x , y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x} ( 6 {x}^{2} + 2 {y}^{2} - 2 x {y}^{2} ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y} ( 6 {x}^{2} + 2 {y}^{2} - 2 x {y}^{2} ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = ( 12 x - 2 {y}^{2} ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} + ( 4 y - 4 x y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{\\nabla } \\, f ( 3 , 3 ) & = ( 12 ( 3 ) - 2 ( 3 {)}^{2} ) \\mathbf{i} + ( 4 ( 3 ) - 4 ( 3 ) ( 3 ) ) \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = 18 \\, \\mathbf{i} - 24 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = \\langle  18 , - 24 \\rangle\\end{array}$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the level curve that passes through the point\n\n$$\n(x_0,y_0)=(3,3)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x_{0}, y_{0}) \\cdot \\langle x - x_{0}, y - y_{0}\\rangle  &= 0 \\\\\n\\nabla f(3, 3) \\cdot \\langle x - 3, y - 3\\rangle  &= 0 \\\\\n\\langle 18,-24\\rangle  \\cdot \\langle x - 3, y - 3\\rangle  &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can leave our answer in this form. Or, if we want to leave our final answer in Cartesian form, we expand the dot product and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18(x - 3) - 24(y - 3) &= 0 \\\\\n18x - 24y + 18 &= 0 \\\\\n3x - 4y + 3 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1940/Source/Images/c5004ab213a6d5c7a2ffee17af5d78e1.png)"] [17592186124174 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^2-3xy+4y=2$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1)$."] [17592186124174 :question/worked-solution "First, if we set\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2-3xy+4y\n$$\n\nour curve can be viewed as the level curve\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2\n$$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1) = f_y(1,1) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(1,1) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1)$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathbf{t} ( x , y ) & = \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y} ( {x}^{2} - 3 x y + 4 y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} - \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x} ( {x}^{2} - 3 x y + 4 y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = ( - 3 x + 4 ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} - ( 2 x - 3 y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathbf{t} ( 1 , 1 ) & = ( - 3 ( 1 ) + 4 ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} - ( 2 ( 1 ) - 3 ( 1 ) ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = 1 \\, \\mathbf{i} - ( - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf i +\\mathbf j$, as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1940/Source/Images/5aaa7192901cc929e9c7816c7f43aa6f.png)"] [17592186124175 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $6xy+3y^2 =7+ y - 3x^2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{j}$?"] [17592186124175 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6xy + 3y^{2} &= 7 + y - 3x^{2} \\\\\n6xy + 3y^{2} - y + 3x^{2} &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 6xy+3y^2 - y + 3x^2\n$$\n\nour curve can be viewed as the level curve\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=7\n$$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x,y) = f_y(x,y) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x,y) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathbf{t} ( x , y ) & = {f}_{y} ( x , y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} - {f}_{x} ( x , y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y} ( 6 x y + 3 {y}^{2} - y + 3 {x}^{2} ) \\, \\mathbf{i} - \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x} ( 6 x y + 3 {y}^{2} - y + 3 {x}^{2} ) \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = ( 6 x + 6 y - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} - ( 6 y + 6 x ) \\, \\mathbf{j}\\end{array}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j}$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t \\cdot \\mathbf j = 0\n$$\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathbf{t} ( x , y ) \\cdot  \\mathbf{j} & = 0 \\\\ ( ( 6 x + 6 y - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} - ( 6 y + 6 x ) \\, \\mathbf{j} ) \\cdot  \\mathbf{j} & = 0 \\\\ - 6 y - 6 x & = 0 \\\\ y & = - x\\end{array}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6xy + 3y^{2} &= 7 + y - 3x^{2} \\\\\n6x(-x) + 3(-x)^{2} &= 7 + (-x) - 3x^{2} \\\\\n- 6x^{2} + 3x^{2} &= 7 - x - 3x^{2} \\\\\nx &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if $x=7$, we get $y=-7$ and the corresponding point is $(7,-7)$."]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
